<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Louis, Prince of Nassau-Hadamar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1590">1590</a>–<a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Louis was a son of John VI of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadamar">Hadamar</a> in the division of his father's lands in 1607. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1629 and brought his territory back to the Catholic faith, serving the emperor as a diplomat, notably at the negotiations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. In 1650 he was raised to the rank of prince. His line died out in 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-louis-prince-of-nassau-hadamar.jpg" alt="John Louis, Prince of Nassau-Hadamar" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Veritas.veritas at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann-ludwig-hadamar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
